# Line-ending policy
#
# This repository is shared across Windows, macOS, and Linux (including WSL).
# Leaving it to git's default autocrlf behavior turns shell scripts into CRLF
# on checkout, and the stray \r right after `#!/usr/bin/env sh` kills the hook
# outright — silently, so it looks like "the hook is installed but blocks
# nothing."
#
# So everything that gets executed is pinned to LF.
#
# The default is LF too. Leaving it at `text=auto` gives Windows checkouts
# CRLF and Linux checkouts LF, so **the same commit produces a different file
# hash per platform**. SHA256SUMS verifies integrity by that hash, so under
# that condition the check stops measuring "is this file intact" and starts
# measuring "which OS are you on." The first CI run caught exactly this
# (85 mismatches, 260807) — pinning only the executable files to LF wasn't
# enough.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Executables — must be LF, regardless of platform
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must stay CRLF
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Hash manifest — a line-ending change makes checksum verification fail outright
SHA256SUMS text eol=lf

# Binaries (no diff/conversion)
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.zip   binary
*.ico   binary

# SnapGene vectors — binary, but without a declaration they were falling under
# `* text=auto eol=lf`.
# 260807: LF normalization folded a two-byte internal CRLF, and the feature
# annotations on four vectors vanished entirely (19/15/16/18 -> 0). The
# sequence itself stayed intact and the parser threw no exception, so primer
# design silently failed to find the CDS.
*.dna   binary
*.xls   binary

